Independent Bernoulli trials
Suppose we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials and let Ai be the event that the ith trial is a
success. If the events {Ai} are independent we say we have a sequence of independent Bernoulli
trials. A standard example occurs when we throw a fair coin n times. Here we can regard this as
n independent Bernoulli trials with “Success” corresponding either to “Heads” or “Tails.”
Another example occurs if we throw a fair die n times. Here we can define “Success” in many
different ways such as “Six” or “Even number” or “Odd number” or “Number less than 5” etc
The next three random variables we look at, the binomial, the geometric and the negative
binomial, arise when we consider independent Bernoulli trials.
